{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HS85WLxyyeFwBnCg61A5DY","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel00.0-uc002-format\n     2→title: \"Format output with columns, color, and human-readable sizes\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator verifies that pkg/format produces correct column layouts, ANSI color\n     6→  sequences, and human-readable size strings. The test exercises each formatting\n     7→  capability in isolation: Columns distributes entries into a grid that fits the terminal\n     8→  width, FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI escape for each file type, the color\n     9→  override mechanism works for --color=always and --color=never, and HumanSize converts\n    10→  byte counts to human-readable strings matching GNU coreutils behavior.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD revision (prd003-format.yaml) and wants to verify\n    16→  that pkg/format satisfies its requirements via unit tests.\n    17→\n    18→flow:\n    19→  - id: F1\n    20→    step: |\n    21→      The operator runs go test ./pkg/format/... which executes the unit tests for\n    22→      pkg/format. Unlike cmd/ packages, pkg/format does not use differential testing\n    23→      because there is no reference binary — it is a library.\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      The Columns test creates a list of 20 filenames and calls Columns(entries, 80).\n    27→      The function distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within\n    28→      80 characters, computing column widths from the longest entry in each column.\n    29→  - id: F3\n    30→    step: |\n    31→      The PadRight and PadLeft tests verify that strings are padded to the specified\n    32→      display width using rune count, not byte count.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The FileTypeColor test calls SetColorEnabled(true), then verifies that each of the\n    36→      eight file types (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device,\n    37→      socket, pipe, regular) returns the correct ANSI escape sequence per GNU ls defaults.\n    38→  - id: F5\n    39→    step: |\n    40→      The color override test calls SetColorEnabled(false) and verifies that FileTypeColor\n    41→      and Reset return empty strings for all file types. It then calls ResetColorEnabled\n    42→      to restore automatic detection.\n    43→  - id: F6\n    44→    step: |\n    45→      The ColorEnabled test passes an os.Pipe writer and verifies it returns false.\n    46→      It passes a bytes.Buffer and verifies it returns false.\n    47→  - id: F7\n    48→    step: |\n    49→      The HumanSize test verifies binary mode (1024-based): HumanSize(1536, binary)\n    50→      returns \"1.5K\", HumanSize(0, binary) returns \"0\". It verifies SI mode (1000-based):\n    51→      HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \"1.0MB\".\n    52→\n    53→touchpoints:\n    54→  - T1: \"pkg/format — Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, displayWidth (prd003-format R1)\"\n    55→  - T2: \"pkg/format — FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled, SetColorEnabled, ResetColorEnabled (prd003-format R2)\"\n    56→  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize (prd003-format R3)\"\n    57→  - T4: \"pkg/sys — IsTerminal, called internally by ColorEnabled\"\n    58→\n    59→success_criteria:\n    60→  - id: S1\n    61→    criterion: Columns(20 filenames, 80) produces a grid where each row fits within 80 characters.\n    62→  - id: S2\n    63→    criterion: PadRight(\"abc\", 10) returns \"abc\" followed by 7 spaces. PadLeft(\"abc\", 10) returns 7 spaces followed by \"abc\".\n    64→  - id: S3\n    65→    criterion: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for all eight file types when color is enabled.\n    66→  - id: S4\n    67→    criterion: SetColorEnabled(false) suppresses all ANSI output. ResetColorEnabled restores automatic detection.\n    68→  - id: S5\n    69→    criterion: ColorEnabled returns false for pipe and non-*os.File writers.\n    70→  - id: S6\n    71→    criterion: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \"1.5K\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \"1.0MB\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \"0\".\n    72→  - id: S7\n    73→    criterion: go test ./pkg/format/... passes with no failures.\n    74→\n    75→out_of_scope:\n    76→  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing is explicitly excluded from pkg/format.\n    77→  - Terminal hyperlinks are not in scope.\n    78→  - East-asian full-width character width computation is deferred.\n    79→\n    80→test_suite: test-rel00.0\n    81→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d47fb82-0e76-4eac-81b0-180fd01d344c","uuid":"5c7f5aa8-9fc8-4e00-8a6a-236449080356","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/291/docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc002-format.yaml","content":"id: rel00.0-uc002-format\ntitle: \"Format output with columns, color, and human-readable sizes\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator verifies that pkg/format produces correct column layouts, ANSI color\n  sequences, and human-readable size strings. The test exercises each formatting\n  capability in isolation: Columns distributes entries into a grid that fits the terminal\n  width, FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI escape for each file type, the color\n  override mechanism works for --color=always and --color=never, and HumanSize converts\n  byte counts to human-readable strings matching GNU coreutils behavior.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD revision (prd003-format.yaml) and wants to verify\n  that pkg/format satisfies its requirements via unit tests.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs go test ./pkg/format/... which executes the unit tests for\n      pkg/format. Unlike cmd/ packages, pkg/format does not use differential testing\n      because there is no reference binary — it is a library.\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      The Columns test creates a list of 20 filenames and calls Columns(entries, 80).\n      The function distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within\n      80 characters, computing column widths from the longest entry in each column.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      The PadRight and PadLeft tests verify that strings are padded to the specified\n      display width using rune count, not byte count.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The FileTypeColor test calls SetColorEnabled(true), then verifies that each of the\n      eight file types (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device,\n      socket, pipe, regular) returns the correct ANSI escape sequence per GNU ls defaults.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The color override test calls SetColorEnabled(false) and verifies that FileTypeColor\n      and Reset return empty strings for all file types. It then calls ResetColorEnabled\n      to restore automatic detection.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The ColorEnabled test passes an os.Pipe writer and verifies it returns false.\n      It passes a bytes.Buffer and verifies it returns false.\n  - id: F7\n    step: |\n      The HumanSize test verifies binary mode (1024-based): HumanSize(1536, binary)\n      returns \"1.5K\", HumanSize(0, binary) returns \"0\". It verifies SI mode (1000-based):\n      HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \"1.0MB\".\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"pkg/format — Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, displayWidth (prd003-format R1)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/format — FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled, SetColorEnabled, ResetColorEnabled (prd003-format R2)\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize (prd003-format R3)\"\n  - T4: \"pkg/sys — IsTerminal, called internally by ColorEnabled\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: Columns(20 filenames, 80) produces a grid where each row fits within 80 characters.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: PadRight(\"abc\", 10) returns \"abc\" followed by 7 spaces. PadLeft(\"abc\", 10) returns 7 spaces followed by \"abc\".\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for all eight file types when color is enabled.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: SetColorEnabled(false) suppresses all ANSI output. ResetColorEnabled restores automatic detection.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: ColorEnabled returns false for pipe and non-*os.File writers.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \"1.5K\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \"1.0MB\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \"0\".\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: go test ./pkg/format/... passes with no failures.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing is explicitly excluded from pkg/format.\n  - Terminal hyperlinks are not in scope.\n  - East-asian full-width character width computation is deferred.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel00.0\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sa9kSMHDRszJJGJYsj7SdK","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd003-format\n     2→title: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n     6→  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n     7→  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n     8→  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n     9→  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n    10→  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n    11→  harder.\n    12→\n    13→  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n    14→  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n    15→  not speculation.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n    19→  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    20→  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Column alignment\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n    27→      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n    28→      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n    29→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n    30→\n    31→  R2:\n    32→    title: ANSI color\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n    35→      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n    36→      - R2.3: |\n    37→          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n    38→      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n    39→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n    40→      - R2.6: |\n    41→          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n    42→          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n    43→          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n    44→          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n    45→          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n    46→      - R2.7: |\n    47→          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n    48→          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n    49→          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n    50→          the override across test cases.\n    51→\n    52→  R3:\n    53→    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R3.1: |\n    56→          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n    57→\n    58→            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n    59→                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R3.2: |\n    62→          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n    63→      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n    64→      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n    65→      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n    66→      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n    67→\n    68→non_goals:\n    69→  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n    70→  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n    71→  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n    72→  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n    73→\n    74→acceptance_criteria:\n    75→  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    76→  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    77→  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    78→  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    79→  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n    80→  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    81→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d47fb82-0e76-4eac-81b0-180fd01d344c","uuid":"522091a3-3ea9-4c60-8d42-631aaacb24ca","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/291/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml","content":"id: prd003-format\ntitle: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n  harder.\n\n  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n  not speculation.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Column alignment\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: ANSI color\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n      - R2.3: |\n          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n      - R2.6: |\n          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n      - R2.7: |\n          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n          the override across test cases.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n\n            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n            }\n      - R3.2: |\n          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}}}
